Basic Authentication:

Client makes a call to server by passing username and password.
Server then validates the credentials with db.
For every subsequent request username and password needs to be passed.


Session Based Authentication:

Client makes a call to server by passing username and password.
Server fetches the credentials from db and creates a session inside the server(may be tomcat)
server sends the sessionid back to the client
and on every subsequent request the client just sends the sessionid to server.
(server need not make db call everytime, session id is enough)
slightly better than basic auth
server is maintaining state(session is stored somewhere on server either on redis/db..)

sessionid is random identifier it doesnt contain user information(username, role, permission, session expiry time)


JWT
Why should server remember anything at all?
jwt token all user details
no session lookup/ no server memory/no redis lookup/no database call
JWT is stateless. as server not maintain any session
Signature verification is done.

JWT token : header, payload, signature
header and payload re base64encoded.
the algo inside header is for signature

signature: encoded header +. + encoded payload + secret key

Signature = HMACSHA256(Base64UrlEncode(header)+"."+ Base64UrlEncode(payload), secretKey)

JWT Flow:
1. token generation : client sends username/password to the server
 server makes db call and fetches the credentials, server validates these credentials
 the server then generates the jwt token containing user details(these are called as claims)
 the token is then signed using a secret key
 the token is then sent back to client
2. client send jwt :from subsequent request client sends the JWT token in Auth header and not username/password
3. Token validation: On server we verify the JWT signature using the same secret key.
    If the signature is valid, the token is trusted and the claims are extracted from payload
4. Access Granted : Based on the extracted claims, the server authorizes the request and processes the API
    so no db calls are required for authentication in every request.

JWT is not part of spring security




Some questions:

Jsessionid and jwt token where are they stored , as a cookie?


JSESSIONID:
Stored in : Browser Cookies (Mandatory default; defaults to URL rewriting only if cookies are disabled)
How is it sent to server: Automatically sent by the browser in the Cookie header on every request.

JWT:
Stored in : Developer's Choice: Browser Cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage
How is it sent to server:
• If in Cookies: Automatically sent.
• If in Storage: Manually attached via JavaScript to the Authorization: Bearer <token> header.